diff --git a/crates/motion/src/drilling/tests_backbore.rs b/crates/motion/src/drilling/tests_backbore.rs
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..a4fa63540
--- /dev/null
+++ b/crates/motion/src/drilling/tests_backbore.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+//! #1480: the back-bore cycle (RS-274/NGC G89 - NIST IR 6556, LinuxCNC's
+//! G-code reference): feed in, dwell at the bottom, feed out at the same
+//! feed. The chain carries the user's dwell and one feed, as the canned
+//! word's single F does.
+
+use super::tests::drill_tool;
+use super::*;
+use crate::cl::{CycleKind, MoveKind};
+use crate::geo2d::Point2;
+
+fn back_bore_params<'a>(tool: &'a crate::tools::Tool, holes: &'a [Hole], dwell: f64) -> CycleParams<'a> {
+    CycleParams {
+        through_clear: crate::drilling::THROUGH_CLEAR,
+        break_through_from: BreakThroughFrom::default(),
+        clearance_z: 15.0,
+        r_z: 10.0,
+        hop_z: None,
+        bottom_z: None,
+        cycle: CycleChoice::BackBore,
+        peck: 0.0,
+        dwell,
+        feed: 500.0,
+        plunge_feed: 100.0,
+        tool,
+        holes,
+    }
+}
+
+fn hole() -> Hole {
+    Hole {
+        xy: Point2::new(10.0, 10.0),
+        radius: 4.0,
+        entry_z: 10.0,
+        floor_z: 4.0,
+        through: false,
+        stepped: false,
+    }
+}
+
+#[test]
+fn back_bore_carries_the_dwell() {
+    let tool = drill_tool(8.0);
+    let hs = [hole()];
+    let t = drill(&back_bore_params(&tool, &hs, 1.5)).unwrap();
+    assert_eq!(t.cycles.len(), 1);
+    let c = &t.cycles[0];
+    assert_eq!(c.kind, CycleKind::BackBore);
+    assert!(
+        (c.dwell - 1.5).abs() < 1e-9,
+        "G89 dwells at the bottom: the cycle record carries the user's 1.5 s, got {}",
+        c.dwell
+    );
+    // No dwell asked, none carried: such a program posts as before.
+    let t0 = drill(&back_bore_params(&tool, &hs, 0.0)).unwrap();
+    assert_eq!(t0.cycles[0].dwell, 0.0);
+}
+
+#[test]
+fn back_bore_feeds_in_and_out_at_the_plunge_feed() {
+    let tool = drill_tool(8.0);
+    let hs = [hole()];
+    for dwell in [0.0, 1.5] {
+        let t = drill(&back_bore_params(&tool, &hs, dwell)).unwrap();
+        let c = &t.cycles[0];
+        let g = &t.moves[c.first_move..];
+        assert_eq!(g[0].kind, MoveKind::Plunge, "feed in");
+        assert_eq!(g[0].feed, Some(100.0));
+        assert!((g[0].to.z - 4.0).abs() < 1e-9, "bores to the floor");
+        assert_eq!(g[1].kind, MoveKind::Feed, "feed out, never a rapid over the bored wall");
+        assert_eq!(
+            g[1].feed,
+            Some(100.0),
+            "the canned word has one F for both directions: the chain feeds out at the feed it fed in at"
+        );
+        assert!((g[1].to.z - 10.0).abs() < 1e-9, "feeds back to R");
+    }
+}
diff --git a/docs/decisions/1480-backbore-dwell.md b/docs/decisions/1480-backbore-dwell.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..8f6e4c002
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/1480-backbore-dwell.md
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+## #1480 - Back bore (G89) carries the dwell and feeds out at the plunge feed
+
+**What was decided.** The drill generator (`camcraft_motion::drilling::drill`)
+now writes the user's dwell into the `Cycle` record of a Back bore hole. It
+also feeds the tool out at the plunge feed, the same feed as the way in.
+Before, the dwell was forced to 0 and the feed out used the cutting feed.
+
+**Why.**
+- G89 is "feed in, dwell, feed out" (RS-274/NGC, NIST IR 6556; LinuxCNC
+  G-code reference). The cycle exists for its dwell, and the user could not
+  get one.
+- A canned G89 (and the G85 that several posts write for a Back bore with no
+  dwell) has one F word. The post takes that F from the first cutting move
+  of the hole's chain, which is the plunge. So the control always fed out at
+  the plunge feed. The chain said the cutting feed. The verifier, the
+  simulation time and the `.cl` dump then disagreed with the machine. The
+  Ream cycle (G85, #1445) already uses the one-feed chain; Back bore now
+  uses the same law.
+- The dwell unit needs no new code. Each post already reads the dwell in
+  seconds from the stream and writes its own P unit, or refuses by name:
+  Fanuc, Mazak, Doosan, Haas and Sinumerik write whole milliseconds; grblHAL,
+  Tormach, LinuxCNC and Mach4 write seconds; Heidenhain writes an explicit
+  dwell block; the lathe posts and the posts with no canned cycle refuse the
+  Back bore. `crates/post/tests/dwell_cycles_modal_p.rs`,
+  `haas_dwell_cycles.rs`, `mach4_post.rs` and `heidenhain_cycles.rs` pin
+  these words (#2350, #2360).
+
+**Considered and dropped.**
+- Keep the feed out at the cutting feed, and make each post write a second F
+  word. A canned cycle has no second F word. A post would have to expand the
+  cycle into G1 moves, which changes every Back bore program.
+- A separate "retract feed" parameter for bore cycles. This is a new
+  parameter and a new command field. It is not necessary for this issue.
+- A new G89 golden for each post in this PR. The posts did not change, and
+  their P words are pinned already. A Sinumerik G89 P pin is the one that is
+  missing; see "Not exercised" in the PR.
+
+**Cost to change later.** Small. The feed out is one line in
+`crates/motion/src/drilling/cycle.rs`, pinned by
+`back_bore_feeds_in_and_out_at_the_plunge_feed`. A Back bore program with no
+dwell posts the same canned block as before on each canned-cycle post. The
+`.cl` chain of a Back bore changes its feed-out feed. On the Heidenhain post,
+which writes the Back bore as an explicit chain, the retract line now has
+the plunge feed.

From fd5767af0704c810dc43ebd669e323d6f37024b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 21:56:22 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(drilling): fine boring cycle (G76) with a required
 shift

Fine boring is a new cycle choice on the drilling operation and a new CL
cycle kind: feed in, dwell, oriented spindle stop, shift off the wall,
rapid out, shift back at R. The shift is a required part of the choice
(whole micrometres along X and Y); there is no default.

The chain makes the shift, so the canned word and the chain cannot
disagree. The post engine reads the shift from the chain (ShiftX, ShiftY)
and refuses the cycle by name for a script that does not declare
fineBoring, and for a chain with no shift. The Haas post declares it and
writes G76 X Y Z R I J P F, one whole block per hole, never Q. The other
17 shipped posts refuse the cycle by name.

A machine file can state spindle.orientation; false refuses a fine bore
with any post. The generator refuses a shift the bar has no room for
((tool dia. - shank dia.) / 2); the command refuses a zero or oversized
shift. The verifier reads the three stopped-spindle rapids with the
stopped bar: the edge zone stays in the cylinder the feed in cut, and the
shank and holder are still read at the shifted position.

Project file, MCP edit_operation and the drill page's Cycle combo carry
the choice and its shift. Programs that do not choose it post
byte-identical.

Stacked on #2823.

Fixes #1479
Fixes #1445

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
